TABLE OF CONTENTS

HBBSCommon.library/--background--
 HBBSCommon.library/--background--                  HBBSCommon.library/--background--

  Description

    Shared library with functions for use by any program.

  Notes

    Last Updated for V1.0

  SeeAlso

    HBBS_InitCommon()
    HBBS_CleanupCommon()

 HBBSCommon.library/FreeStr                         HBBSCommon.library/FreeStr
  void FreeStr( register __a0 char *str )


    Description

      This function will call FreeVec() on the string pointer passed
      to it if the string pointer is not NULL


    Inputs

      str - pointer to a string to be freed that was previously allocated with
            AllocVec() or another HBBSCommon.library function such as DupStr().


    Returns

      None

 HBBSCommon.library/DupStr                          HBBSCommon.library/DupStr
  char *DupStr( register __a0 char *str )

    Description

      This function copies the string passed to it, and returns a pointer
      to the newly allocated copy.

    Inputs

      str pointer to a string to be duplicated

    Returns

      Pointer to a copy of the string passed to it, or NULL if out
      of memory

 HBBSCommon.library/FreeAndSet                      HBBSCommon.library/FreeAndSet
  char *FreeAndSet( register __a0 char **varname , register __a1 char *newstr )

    Description

      FreeStr()'s varname and then DupStr()'s newstr and stores result in
      varname.

      Varname *must* be initialised to NULL or to a AllocVec()ed pointer


    Inputs

      varname address of a pointer to a string
      newstr  pointer to a string to be copied

    Results

      returns varname

  V_BOOL CheckBoolean( register __a0 char *str )

    Description

      Checks str against boolean words: YES, ON, TRUE
      and returns the boolean equivalent

    Inputs

      str pointer to a string containing a boolean word

    Returns

      TRUE if string is equal (case insensitve) to one of the boolean words
      listed above, FALSE otherwise

  V_ERROR NewStrNode( register __a0 char *str ,
          register __a1 struct List *list )

    Description

      Allocates a new node with ln_Name set to a copy of str, and appends
      it to the list specified in list

    Inputs

      str pointer to a string, or NULL
      list  list to add the newly created node to

    Returns

      TYPE_MEMORY if there was a memory error while duplicating the string or
      allocating a new node
      ERR_NO_ERR if all was ok

    See Also

      HBBS_FreeNode()
      HBBS_FreeListNodes()
      FreeStrList()

  void UpperCase( register __a0 char *str )

    Description

      converts str into uppercase

    Inputs

      str pointer to a string

    Returns

      none

  char *upcase( register __a0 char *str )

    Description

      Duplicates str and converts the copy to uppercase leaving str unmodified

    Inputs

      str pointer to a string

    Returns

      a pointer to a new string containing a uppercase version of str or
      NULL of there was a memory error

    Notes

      Don't forget to FreeStr() the returned string

  short position( register __a0 char *substr , register __a1 char *str )

    Description

      checks for the occurence of substr in str and returns the character
      offset from the start of the string where substr was found
      Case sensitive

    Inputs

      substr  pointer to a sub string
      str pointer to a string possibly containing substr

    Returns

      -1 if substr was not found in str
      or the character index substr starts at in str starting from 0

    See Also

      iposition()

  short iposition( register __a0 char *substr , register __a1 char *str )

    Description

      Same as position() but case in-sensitive, see iposition for
      inputs and returns

    Returns

      as per position() but may return -2 if it runs out of memory

    See also

      position()

  void strfcpy( register __a0 char *dest , register __a1 char *source , register __d0 int from )

    Description

      copies source to dest starting at offset specified in from

    Bugs

      Do not use, instead use strcpy(dest,source+from)
      as it does the same thing and is quicker.

  void stripcr( register __a0 char *s )

    Description

      removes all \r or \n charaters from the end of a string until
      the string is empty or it finds a different character
      The string may contain \r's or \n's in the middle still...


    Inputs

      s   string to remove \r's and \n's from

    Returns

      None

  UBYTE *StripComments( register __a0 char *s )

    Description

      Removes the first ; and everyting after it from the string s

      Used to remove comments from config files.

      Works by changing the first ; to a \0 (moves the null terminator)

    Inputs

      s   pointer to a string

    Returns

      a pointer to the start of the comments in string s

  void StripSpaces( register __a0 char *s )

    Description

      Removes all trailing spaces from s

    Inputs

      s   pointer to a string

    Returns

      none

    Notes

      This is handy for removing end of line blanks from config files
      and strings input by users

    See Also

      StripComments()

  ULONG __asm __saveds HBBS_GetAmigaTime( void )

    Description

      performs same task as ReadBattClock() from the battclock resource but does
      not require you to open and devices or libs etc.

    Returns

      see ReadBattClock() in battclock.resource

  int __asm __saveds strNIcmp(register __a0 char *s1, register __a1 char *s2,register __d0 int n)


    Description

      compares the first n bytes of a string to another string.  see stricmp() for return codes


  void __asm __saveds replace(register __a0 char *dest,register __a1 char *compare,register __a2 char *from,register __a3 char *to)


    Description

      replaces all occurences of "compare" in "from" with "to" and stores the result in "dest"

  BOOL __asm __saveds GetParams(register __a0 char *dest,register __a1 char *source)

    Description

      copy everything after the "=" in "source" in "dest"

      i.e pass it "Item=Paramaters..." as the source and dest would contain "paramaters..."

    Inputs

      dest - must point to an allocated section of memory to store the paramaters in.
      source - pointer to a string

    Returns

      TRUE if anything was copied to "dest"

  BOOL __asm __saveds GetItem(register __a0 char *dest,register __a1 char *source)

    Description

      Same as GetParams() except it copies everything before the "=" in "source" to "dest"

    Inputs

      dest - must point to an allocated section of memory to store the paramaters in.
      source - pointer to a string

    Returns

      TRUE if anything was copied to "dest"


  void __asm __saveds GetNumber(register __a0 int *num,register __a1 char *item)

    Description

      copies the last n digits from item into a buffer (internal to function) upto
      a maximum of 5 digits and converts the buffer to an integer and stores it
      in the address pointed to by "*num"

    Inputs

      *num - pointer to a place to store an integer
      *item - pointer to a string

    Example


      int mynum;

      GetNumber(&mynum,"thisisanumber123456");

      mynum would then contain 23456


  ULONG __asm __saveds  SafePutToPort(register __a0 struct Message *message, register __a1 STRPTR portname)

    Description

      sends "message" to a named port "portname".  Checks for existance of the portname, sends the message in
      a Forbid()/Permit() pair.

    Inputs

      Message - pointer to a struct Message with correctly filled fields.
      portname - string containing name of port.

    Returns

      NULL if port does not exist, or the address of the port otherwise.

  struct Message __asm __saveds *SendMessage(register __a0 struct Message *Msg,register __a1 char *MPortName)

    Description

      Same as SafePutToPort() except that if the message you are passing has a mn_ReplyPort that is not NULL
      it will wait for the reply to the message.

    Returns

      NULL  if port does not exist.
      1 if message was sent but no reply port was specified
      the address of the Message that was sent back. (i.e. the return from GetMsg(Msg->mn_ReplyPort)

  ULONG __asm __saveds HBBS_NodesInList(register __a0 struct List *list)

    Description

      Counts the number of nodes in the "list" specified and returns the amount.

  void __asm __saveds FreeStrList(register __a0 V_STRINGLIST strlist)

    Description

      free all strings in "strlist" and removes all nodes from the list.
      then frees the nodes and the list itself..

    Inputs

      strlist - a list that has been allocated with AllocVec() and it's nodes created with
                AllocVec(), such as those created by HBBS_CreateList() and HBBS_CreateNode()

    Notes

      to be on the safe side set the strlist value that you pass this function to NULL after
      you have called it.  e.g.

        struct List *mylist;

        .
        .

        FreeStrList(mylist);
        mylist=NULL;


  struct BBSGlobalData __asm __saveds *HBBS_GimmeBBS( void )

    Description

      Requests a pointer to the main BBS structures

    Returns

      pointer to a struct BBSGlobalData

    Notes

      See structures.h for readonly areas of this structure.

  void __asm __saveds  HBBS_SetBBS(register __a0 struct BBSGlobalData *B)

    Description

      ** PRIVATE **  DO NOT USE!


  struct Node __asm __saveds  *GetNode(register __a0 struct List *lh,register __d0 UWORD n)


    Description/Returns

      returns a pointer to the "n"'th node in the list "lh".  Make sure the list actually has n nodes
      though or a crash will result.

    Inputs

      n - node number.  First node in a list is 0, not 1.

  UWORD __asm __saveds GetNodeNum(register __a0 struct List *lh,register __a1 struct Node *node)


    Description

      checks the list "lh" for the "node" specified and returns its position in the list

    Returns

      Position of "node" in list "lh", first node is 0.

    Notes

      "node" MUST be a node in list "lh".

  struct NodeData __asm __saveds  *HBBS_NodeDataPtr(register __d0 short nodenum)

    Description

      requests the NodeData structure for a given node

    Inputs

      nodenum - node number, starting at 0.

    Returns

      pointer to a NodeData structure.  Note. the node may be off-line. check
      NodeData->Status before using any other parts of the structure.

      (see defines.h/stat_#?)

  V_ERROR __asm __saveds HBBS_LogError(register __a0 V_STRING filename,register __d0 V_ERROR errnum,register __a1 V_STRING string,register __d1 V_ERROR errtype)

    Description

      Write an error message to a logfile.

    Inputs

      filename - string containing name of log file.
      errnum - one of ERR_#? (see defines.h)
      string - the error message to write to a file
      errtype - one of TYPE_#? (see defines.h)

    Returns

      errnum

    Example

      HBBS_LogError(N_ND->NodeSettings.ModemLogFile,ERR_GERERAL,"Error initalising modem",TYPE_INFO);

  struct CfgFileData __asm __saveds *HBBS_LoadConfig(register __a0 UBYTE *filename,register __d0 ULONG Flags)

    Description

      Loads the items in a config file to memory.

    Inputs

      filename - name of config file to load, e.g. progdir:mydoor.cfg
      flags - one of LCFG_#? (see defines.h) normally LCFG_NONE

    Returns

      NULL if config file could not be loaded.
      Pointer to a struct ConfigFileData otherwise.

    Notes

      Must be paired with a HBBS_FlushConfig() if the loading of the config suceeded.

      See the section on config files in the main HBBS docs for config file format.
      (in short, each item on seperate line, ;'s are comments. items take the form of Item=Paramater
      items must start at beginning of each new line, blank lines are fine.

    See Also

      HBBS_FlushConfig(), HBBS_GetSetting(), HBBS_LoadConfigQuick(), HBBS_SaveConfig()
      HBBS_CreateConfig(), HBBS_RemoveCfgItem(), HBBS_AddCfgItem()

  struct CfgFileData __asm __saveds *HBBS_LoadConfigQuick(register __a0 UBYTE *filename,register __d0 ULONG Flags,register __a1 UBYTE *PatternStr)

    Description

      Same as HBBS_LoadConfig() except that it only loads items that match the pattern specified.

    Inputs

      same as in HBBS_LoadConfig() except:
      PatternStr - normal amigados pattern. e.g. FR_#?

    Notes

      This is usefull if you only want one ore two items from a config file.
      For instance. When HBBS is searching command config files for door information
      users the <doorname>#? as a pattern.  e.g.  CheckFiles#?

      This is great for speeding HBBS and your doors up.

  void  __asm __saveds  HBBS_FlushConfig(register __a0 struct CfgFileData *cfgfile)

    Description

      Flushes a config and it's items from memory.

    Inputs

      cfgfile - pointer to a config file created or loaded using HBBS_CreateConfig() or HBBS_LoadConfig()

  V_BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_SaveConfig(register __a0 struct CfgFileData *cfgfile)

    Description

      Saves a config file to disk.

      The file uses is the same as the one passed to HBBS_LoadConfig() or HBBS_CreateConfig()

    Inputs

      cfgfile - pointer to a config file created or loaded using HBBS_CreateConfig() or HBBS_LoadConfig()

    Returns

      TRUE if save suceeded.

  V_SMALLNUM __asm __saveds  HBBS_GetSetting(register __a0 struct CfgFileData *cfgfile,register __a1 void **result,register __d0 V_FLAGS optiontype,register __a2 V_STRING optionstr,register __d1 V_BOOL multi)

    Description

      Gets a config item from a config and stores the paramater of the item in "result"

    Inputs

      cfgfile - pointer to a config file created or loaded using HBBS_CreateConfig() or HBBS_LoadConfig()
      result - pointer to store results in, depends on type of variable being requested.
      optiontype - one of TYPE_#? (see types.h)
      optionstr - the option name as it would appear in the config file.  (see notes below)
      multi - either OPT_MULTI or OPT_SINGLE.  (see notes below)

    Returns

      Config items found.  Only 0 or 1 if OPT_SINGLE is used, if OPT_MULTI is used it'll return the amount of
      nodes allocated in the list.

    Notes

      if OPT_MULTI is used then the optionstr should not contain the _XX. i.e. if you want to get Item_1=blah
      and Item_2=more blah then just set optiontype to "Item".

      Currently the one two variable types that support OPT_MULTI are TYPE_STRINGLIST or TYPE_PATHLIST.

    Examples

      See HBBS:Developer/Examples/ConfigFiles/Configs.c

  struct CfgFileData __asm __saveds *HBBS_CreateConfig(register __a0 UBYTE *filename)

    Description

      Creates a config file.

    Inputs

      filename - filename of config file to use.

    Returns

      NULL or a pointer to a struct CfgFileData that you should pass to other config file related
      functions.

    Notes

      Calling this does not write the config file to disk. it just allocated a new config file
      structure in memory.  Save the config file using HBBS_SaveConfig(), add items use HBBS_AddCfgItem()

  BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_RemoveCfgItem(register __a0 struct CfgFileData *cfgfile,register __a1 UBYTE *itemname)

    Description

      Remove items from a config file in memory
      (note: this does not affect, the file on the disk)

    Inputs

      cfgfile - pointer to a config file created or loaded using HBBS_CreateConfig() or HBBS_LoadConfig()
      itemname - An item to remove, wildcards are allowed here too..

    Returns

      TRUE if items removed.

  BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_AddCfgItem(register __a0 struct CfgFileData *cfgfile,register __a1 UBYTE *itemname,register __a2 UBYTE *params)

    Description

      Adds itmes to a configfile (structure in memory)

    Inputs

      cfgfile - pointer to a config file created or loaded using HBBS_CreateConfig() or HBBS_LoadConfig()
      itemname - An item to add
      params - parameters for the item. in the format of a null terminated string.

    Returns

      TRUE if atem added.

    Notes

      if the item was already in the config file it will be changed to the new value.

  V_BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_AddCfgItemQuick(register __a0 struct CfgFileData *cfgfile,register __a1 char *ItemName, register __a2 char *Params)

    Description

      Adds an item to a config file quickly, does not overwrite old values.

      e.g. Use this if you are creating a config file to save and have not called HBBS_LoadConfig()

      Otherwise it's the same as HBBS_AddCfgItem()

  void __asm __saveds HBBS_ResetNodeData(register __a0 struct NodeData *node)

    Description

      ** PRIVATE **  DO NOT USE!

  void __asm __saveds  HBBS_rterror(register __a0 char *str)

    Description

      display an error message using reqtools.  Note that this will halt operation of a node until the sysop
      clicks the "OK" button on the requester.

    Inputs

      str - error message string


  LONG __asm __saveds  HBBS_RunDOSCMD(register __a0 char *command,register __d0 BOOL ASYNC)

    ** DO NOT USE THIS FUNCTION ANY MORE! **

    See HBBS_DosCommand() instead!

  void __asm __saveds HBBS_DoErrorMessage(register __d0 ULONG num,register __d1 ULONG node,register __a0 char *errstr)

    Description

      spawns the external error message program.  i.e. display an error message without waiting for
      the sysop to click ok.

      The message must be defined in HBBS:Doors/System/ErrorMessage/ErrorMessage.txt and a define
      value must be created in errors.h.

      This is not really meant for gereral use.

    Inputs

      num - error number, see errors.h
      node - current node number
      errstr - if the message string defined in the ErrorMessage.txt file contains @E@ it will be
               replaced with this string.

  BOOL __asm __saveds AssignOK(register __a0 char *checkme )

    Description

      Pass it an assign to check and it will..

    Inputs

      checkme - a string containing an assign to check.

    Returns

      if the volume is mounted, the assign exists then it will return TRUE.

    Notes

      This function is so you can check paths before open files in them to prevent
      "Please Insert Volume xxx in any drive" requesters from appearing.

      Copes with progdir: too (which is not in the dos device list)

  BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_InitCommon( void )

    Description

      Call this immeditaly after opening the HBBSCommon.library with DOS/OpenLibrary()

      If it fails you must still call HBBS_CleanUpCommon().

      This function can only be caled when HBBS's control program is running.
      (no nodes need be started however)

    Returns

      TRUE of all is OK.

    Notes

      Must be paired with HBBS_CleanUpCommon()

  void __asm __saveds HBBS_CleanUpCommon( void )

    Description

      Must be called imediatly prior to DOS/CloseLibrary() when closing the HBBSCommon.library

  struct TimerData __asm __saveds *SubmitTimer(register __a0 struct TimerSetupData *TSD, register __d0 ULONG Seconds,register __d1 ULONG MicroSeconds )

    Description

      Submit a timer which you can wait for using the message port TimerSetupData->TimerPort or use CheckTimer() below

    Inputs

      TSD - a pointer to a struct TimerSetupData allocated with InitTimer()
      seconds - seconds to wait
      microseconds - microseconds to wait

    Returns

      struct TimerData. should be freed using AbortTimer()

    SeeAlso

      InitTimer(), CleanupTimer(), CheckTimer(), AbortTimer()

  void __asm __saveds AbortTimer(register __a0 struct TimerSetupData *TSD, register __a1 struct TimerData *CTD)

    Description

      Aborts a timer and frees the memory allocated by SubmitTimer()

    Inputs

      TSD - a pointer to a struct TimerSetupData allocated with InitTimer()
      CTD - a pointer to a struct TimerData allocated by SubmitTimer()

  BOOL __asm __saveds CheckTimer(register __a0 struct TimerSetupData *TSD, register __a1 struct TimerData *CTD)

    Description

      Checks a previously submitted timer

    Inputs

      TSD - a pointer to a struct TimerSetupData allocated with InitTimer()
      CTD - a pointer to a struct TimerData allocated by SubmitTimer()

    Returns

      TRUE if timer has completed.  If this function returns true you should set the variable
      passed as CTD to NULL as it has now been freed and should NOT be referenced again.

      FALSE if timer has not completed yet.

  void __asm __saveds CleanupTimer( register __a0 struct TimerSetupData *TSD )

    Description

      Cleans up a timer created with IntTimer()

    Inputs

      TSD - a pointer to a struct TimerSetupData allocated with InitTimer()

    Notes

      set the variable passed as TSD to NULL after calling this.

  struct TimerSetupData __asm __saveds *InitTimer( void )

    Description

      Create a timer.

      Pass the returned variable to other timer related functions.

      Mut be paired with a call to CleanupTimer()

    See Also

      SubmitTimer(), CheckTimer(), AbortTimer(), CleanupTimer()

  BOOL __asm __saveds PathOK(register __a0 char *str)

    Description

      Checks that a path exists.

    Returns

      TRUE if path exists

  V_BOOL __asm __saveds  HBBS_LoadUser(register __d0 V_BIGNUM fileoffset,
                                       register __d1 V_BIGNUM ID,
                                       register __a0 char *handle,
                                       register __a1 char *realname,
                                       register __a2 struct UserData *User,
                                       register __d2 ULONG Flags);

    Description

      Loads a users information from the user data file into memory.

    Inputs

      FileOffset - Load a specific section of the user data file
      ID - UserID of user to load
      handle - handle of user to load
      realname - realname of user to load
      user - previously allocated memory to store the user information in.
      Flags - currently supports the following options (see defines.h)
              (logical or the flags together to specify more than one at
              a time, specify LOADUSER_NONE if you're not using any
              other flags)

        LOADUSER_NONE             - Check all users.
        LOADUSER_SKIPOVERWRITABLE - Don't return user accounts with a ->Status
                                    of USER_OVERWRITEABLE.
        LOADUSER_SKIPID           - If you are searching for a handle, and
                                    want to skip a particular ID then
                                    specify this flag, and the
                                    ID and handle paramaters.

                                    The function HBBS_HandleInUse() uses this
                                    flag.

    Notes

      Specify only one of FileOffset, ID, handle or realname.  set unused
      inputs to NULL or 0.

      The LOADUSER_SKIPOVERWRITABLE flag does not apply to FileOffset searching
      mode, specify LOADUSER_NONE


      handle and realname have not been implemented as of A17.

    Example

      struct UserData myuser;

      if (HBBS_LoadUser(3,NULL,NULL,&myuser))
      {
        .
        . do stuff with data in myuser
        .
      }

  V_BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_ValidUserHandle(register __a0 char *userhandle, register __a1 struct UserData *FillUser)

    Description

      Checks to see if a handle is already in use.  Will also return handles of users that
      have been deleted.  If a user has a status of 'O' (overwritable) it will NOT be returned.

      So you can also use this to re-instate deleted users etc..

    Inputs

      userhandle - name of users handle to find.
      FillUser - if not NULL then the information about the user to be located is loaded into the address pointed to here.

    Results

      TRUE if user was found.

    Example

      struct UserData myuser;

      if (HBBS_ValidUserHandle("Hydra",&myuser))
      {
        .
        . do stuff with data in myuser
        .
      }

  V_BIGNUM __asm __saveds HBBS_FindTotalUsers( void )

    Description

      Returns the amount of users (deleted invalid or otherwise) in the user.data

      Use this rather than BBSGlobalData->TotalUsers

    Returns

      the amount of users

  BOOL __asm __saveds LoadPrivateData( void )

    Description

      ** PRIVATE **  DO NOT USE!

  void __asm __saveds UpdatePrivateData ( void )

    Description

      ** PRIVATE **  DO NOT USE!

  BOOL __asm __saveds HBBS_AddUser(register __a0 struct UserData *user)

    Description

      Add a user to the user.data file.

    Inputs

      user - a correctly completed struct UserData structure. See HBBS:Source/Doors_System/NewUser/Main.C

    Returns

      TRUE if user added OK.

